# Abstract

Public ferry transportation plays an important role in connecting urban communities, supporting tourism and providing access to recreational destinations. Effective ferry operations require an understanding of passenger demand patterns, seasonal variations and peak travel periods.

This research presents an exploratory data analysis of Toronto Island Ferry ticket sales and redemption records using historical timestamp-based data. The primary objective is to identify temporal demand patterns, compare ticket sales and redemptions, examine seasonal variations and develop data-driven insights that can support operational planning.

The project uses Microsoft Power BI, Power Query and Data Analysis Expressions (DAX) to transform and analyse ticket records. The analytical framework includes hourly ticket sales, hourly redemptions, net ticket difference, peak demand identification and an off-season utilization index.

An interactive dashboard was developed featuring key performance indicators, time-series analysis, an hourly demand heatmap, peak versus off-peak comparison, seasonal demand analysis and interactive filtering.

Preliminary dashboard observations identify 12:00–13:00 as the highest-demand hourly window in the displayed analysis. The seasonal comparison also indicates stronger demand during summer than during the colder seasons. These observations require validation against the complete underlying dataset before being treated as statistically conclusive.

The research demonstrates how business intelligence tools can transform transportation ticketing records into operational insights and support evidence-based decision-making.

**Keywords:** Toronto Ferry, Exploratory Data Analysis, Power BI, Ticket Sales, Ticket Redemptions, Seasonal Demand, Transportation Analytics, Business Intelligence.

