# 1. Introduction

Urban transportation systems are essential to the economic, social and recreational activities of cities. Ferry transportation provides an important connection between mainland areas and island communities, particularly where water-based travel is necessary.

Toronto Island Ferry services connect the mainland at Jack Layton Ferry Terminal with Centre Island, Hanlan's Point and Ward's Island.

Demand for ferry services can vary considerably according to the time of day, day of the week, season, holidays and weather conditions. Understanding these variations is important for effective scheduling, resource allocation and passenger experience management.

The City of Toronto operates ferry services throughout the year, with seasonal changes in routes and schedules. Summer services generally operate across multiple island routes, while winter services operate with a reduced service pattern.

These operational variations make Toronto Island Ferry an appropriate case study for applying exploratory data analysis and business intelligence techniques.

This project focuses on analysing historical ticket sales and redemption records to identify demand patterns and present the results through an interactive Power BI dashboard.

# 2. Research Problem

Transportation operators require reliable information about when passenger demand is highest and how it changes throughout the year.

Without systematic analysis, identifying recurring demand patterns and seasonal differences can be challenging.

The main research problem addressed in this project is:

