## 6.4 Hourly Aggregation

The original dataset contains observations at 15-minute intervals.

To calculate hourly demand, records were grouped into hourly intervals using the date and hour of the timestamp.

Four 15-minute intervals therefore contribute to a complete hour when all four records are present.

Average hourly ticket demand was calculated across observed date-hour combinations.

This approach avoids treating individual 15-minute observations as complete hourly totals.

# 7. Key Performance Indicators

Five primary KPIs were developed.

## 7.1 Tickets Sold per Hour

Measures the average number of tickets sold during an observed hourly interval.

Formula:

Average Hourly Sales = Total Tickets Sold / Number of Observed Date-Hour Combinations

This KPI helps identify recurring periods of higher ticket demand.

## 7.2 Tickets Redeemed per Hour

Measures the average number of tickets redeemed during an observed hourly interval.

Formula:

Average Hourly Redemptions = Total Tickets Redeemed / Number of Observed Date-Hour Combinations

This indicator provides information about ticket redemption activity.

## 7.3 Net Ticket Difference

Measures the difference between total tickets sold and total tickets redeemed.

Formula:

Net Ticket Difference = Total Tickets Sold − Total Tickets Redeemed

This metric identifies the difference between ticket sales and redemption counts.

It should not be interpreted as a direct measure of passengers physically entering or leaving the terminal.

## 7.4 Peak Demand Window

Identifies the hourly interval with the highest average ticket sales.

The dashboard uses hourly demand rankings to identify periods of elevated ticket activity.

The current dashboard identifies 12:00–13:00 as the highest-demand hourly window in the displayed analysis.

## 7.5 Off-Season Utilization Index

The proposed index compares average hourly sales outside summer with average hourly sales during summer.

Formula:

Off-Season Utilization Index = (Average Off-Season Hourly Sales / Average Summer Hourly Sales) × 100

An index of 40 indicates that average off-season hourly sales are 40% of the summer reference level.

This is a relative demand index, not a measure of vessel capacity utilization.

# 8. Exploratory Data Analysis

The Power BI dashboard includes several visualizations to examine the temporal and seasonal characteristics of ferry ticket demand.

## 8.1 Hourly Ticket Demand Analysis

A line chart was developed to compare ticket sales and redemptions over time.

This visualization helps identify long-term demand patterns, fluctuations and periods of increased activity.

The chart allows users to examine historical demand across the selected reporting period.

## 8.2 Hourly Passenger Demand Heatmap

A matrix-based heatmap was developed to compare average hourly ticket sales across the days of the week.

The visualization uses conditional formatting to distinguish lower and higher demand periods.

This helps identify recurring combinations of days and hours that may require additional operational attention.

## 8.3 Peak and Off-Peak Analysis

The dashboard includes an hourly demand comparison and a classification of demand periods.

Based on the preliminary hourly ranking, the 10:00–15:00 interval was classified as a provisional higher-demand period.

The highest individual hourly window displayed in the dashboard was 12:00–13:00.

These classifications should be evaluated separately by season and day type before being adopted as permanent scheduling rules.

## 8.4 Seasonal Demand Analysis

A clustered column chart was developed to compare average hourly sales and redemptions across four seasons.

The preliminary dashboard visualization indicates stronger demand during summer compared with winter, spring and fall.

This pattern is consistent with the seasonal operating context of Toronto Island Ferry services.

Exact seasonal averages and percentage differences should be calculated from the validated dataset before final publication.

