# 10. Operational Recommendations

Based on the analytical framework and preliminary dashboard observations, the following recommendations are proposed.

## 10.1 Demand-Based Scheduling

Ferry scheduling should consider recurring high-demand periods rather than relying exclusively on fixed operating timetables.

Historical hourly demand can help identify periods that warrant further operational review.

## 10.2 Seasonal Resource Planning

Seasonal demand differences should be considered when planning staff allocation, vessel availability and service frequency.

Higher summer demand and comparatively lower winter demand may require different operating arrangements.

## 10.3 Weekend Demand Monitoring

Weekends and holidays should be analysed separately because passenger demand may differ from ordinary weekdays.

Additional service planning should be supported by recurring demand evidence.

## 10.4 Passenger Congestion Management

Identifying recurring high-demand periods can support terminal staffing, queue management and passenger communication.

## 10.5 Continuous Performance Monitoring

A regularly refreshed dashboard can help operational teams monitor changes in ticket sales and redemptions.

Future implementations may incorporate weather, holiday calendars, ferry schedules and vessel capacity information.

# 11. Limitations of the Study

The study has several limitations.

1. Ticket sales do not necessarily represent the exact time at which passengers travel.
2. Ticket redemption counts do not independently identify the direction of passenger movement.
3. The available dataset does not provide complete vessel capacity or occupancy information.
4. Weather conditions and special events are not included in the current analytical model.
5. Missing records and unusual observations require further quantitative validation.
6. Historical averages may conceal differences between weekdays, weekends and individual seasons.
7. The current Power BI implementation is a historical analytical dashboard rather than a continuously connected real-time operational system.
8. The off-season utilization index requires final calculation validation.

These limitations should be considered when interpreting the results and developing operational recommendations.

